WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.220
MINHAZ KAZI: Welcome
to "DataVis DevTalk."

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.710
I'm your host, Minhaz
Kazi, Developer Advocate

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.330
for Data Studio.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.850
In this video, we'll talk
about the connector manifest

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.120
and why it is important to have
the right information there.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:12.950
Let's get started.

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:16.331
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.150
Your connector's manifest
is a special JSON file

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:24.210
in your Apps Script project
that specifies certain metadata

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.160
about your connector.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.960
Apps Script needs some
of this information

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.300
to run the script successfully.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.490
Data Studio also needs
certain information

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:36.150
from the manifest to display
and execute the connector.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.580
You can find out more
about Apps Script manifests

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.920
in general in the link below.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.260
Apps Script automatically
creates and updates

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.840
the project manifest as
you create your connector

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.180
and make changes in
the Apps Script editor.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.650
However, there are
a few properties

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.770
that you'll need to update
manually for your connector.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:54.720
First is the Data
Studio property.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.430
All information
about your connector

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.680
will be added under
this property.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.960
Some of this information
is mandatory.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.031
Your connector's name is
limited to 28 characters.

00:01:04.031 --> 00:01:06.030
You'll need to provide
the name of the developer

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.900
or the organization, as well as
the URL for the organization.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.230
You should have a logo or
icon for your connector

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:15.020
that should be 40
pixels by 40 pixels.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:18.330
That add-on URL should be a link
to a page that is preferably

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.790
hosted on your
domain and explains

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.730
what your connector
does and how to use it.

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.710
This is the link
users will go to when

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:29.010
they click on the Learn More
button on your connector card.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:32.700
The support URL should link to a
support page for the connector.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.890
Users should be able
to report issues

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.720
with the connector at this link.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.000
You'll also need to provide a
description for your connector.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.120
This will be visible in the
connector configuration page.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.280
Links in this description
will be clickable.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:47.910
It is always a good
idea to provide

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:50.640
all information or
instructions necessary to have

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.070
a basic understanding
of the connector

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.590
and how to use it
in the description.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.170
This mandatory information
is required for Data Studio

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:01.620
to render the connector
interface on different screens.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.600
This information
also helps the user

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.220
to know more about
your connector

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.976
and explore on their own.

00:02:06.976 --> 00:02:08.850
Then we have some certain
optional properties

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.919
under the Data Studio property.

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:12.810
These aren't required
if you're building

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:14.992
a connector for a limited use.

00:02:14.992 --> 00:02:16.950
However, if you plan to
publish your connector,

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:21.480
you must include this optional
information in the manifest.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:22.950
This information
helps to showcase

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.930
the connector in
the gallery, and let

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:27.420
the users quickly search
for connectors and filter

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.220
based on certain criteria.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:33.120
The short description is
limited to 114 characters.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.490
If your connector is published,
this short description

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:38.970
will be visible in the gallery
card for your connector.

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:41.880
Use this to briefly describe
what your connector does.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.710
Users will view this
short description

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.190
when they browse through the
connectors in the gallery.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.750
You'll need to provide
links to your privacy policy

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:52.110
and terms of service, list
authentication type, and fee

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.310
type for your connector.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.870
If you want to provide a
template for your users,

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.050
you can list it in the manifest.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.480
The template will provide
a seamless experience

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.600
for your users since they will
start with the template instead

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.510
of a blank dashboard when
they use your connector.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.310
We will have a
separate video focusing

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.080
on the template experience.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.650
One important entry in the
manifest is the sources.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.720
Your connector might connect
to one or more sources.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.050
For example, if you create
a Google Fit connector,

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.870
your source for data
would be Google Fit.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.270
To add the source's
property, you first

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.660
must ensure that the
source information exists

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.510
in our source registry
on the Data Studio

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:33.030
open-source repository.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:35.370
If your source does
not exist there,

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:38.850
you can add it yourself by
sending us a pull request.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.460
Sources act as metadata
when users search

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.260
for a specific connector.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.120
More information about
this can be found

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.910
in the link in the description.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:50.280
There are two additional
properties at root level

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.560
that you will need
to be aware of.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.520
If you're using the Apps Script
URL fetch app call to fetch

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:00.330
data from HTTP endpoints, you
should list all the endpoints

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:03.750
under the URL fetch
whitelist property.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:06.660
This is mandatory when you're
publishing your connector.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.970
You will not be able to
make calls to endpoints

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:10.990
outside of this list.

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:14.110
So be careful to list
all applicable endpoints,

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:17.490
including the ones
required for authorization.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.320
The second property
is OAuth scopes.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.079
Apps Script
automatically determines

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:25.260
what scopes a script needs by
scanning its code for function

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:26.910
calls that require them.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:30.090
For most scripts, this is
sufficient and saves you time.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:32.070
However, for advanced
use cases, like when

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:34.620
you're using a service
account to access data,

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.500
you might need to explicitly
define your scopes.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:40.350
That covers the basics for
your connector manifest.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:42.990
As we add more features to
Data Studio and community

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:45.000
connectors, more
options might be

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.062
available in the
manifest in the future.

00:04:48.062 --> 00:04:49.770
Make sure to check
the manifest reference

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:52.890
link below when you're
developing your own connector.

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:54.660
You can always go to
our developer site

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:57.510
at
developers.google.com/datastudio

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:59.010
for more information.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:01.560
If you liked this video,
stay tuned on the GCP channel

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.427
for more Data Studio content.

00:05:03.427 --> 00:05:04.260
Thanks for watching.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.610
[MUSIC PLAYING]

